### What is coarse-grained locking?

Coarse-grained locking uses a single lock to protect all shared state. Every operation acquires the same lock, so only one thread can execute at a time.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class CoarseBank {
    private final Object bankLock = new Object();
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> accounts = new HashMap<>();

    public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        synchronized (bankLock) {
            BigDecimal fromBal = accounts.getOrDefault(from, BigDecimal.ZERO);
            BigDecimal toBal = accounts.getOrDefault(to, BigDecimal.ZERO);
            accounts.put(from, fromBal.subtract(amount));
            accounts.put(to, toBal.add(amount));
        }
    }
    // Simple! No deadlock! But SAV2001→CUR3001 blocks FD4001→SAV5001
    // even though they touch different accounts
}
```

### What is fine-grained locking?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class FineBank {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, ReentrantLock> locks = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, BigDecimal> accounts = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    private ReentrantLock getLock(String accountId) {
        return locks.computeIfAbsent(accountId, k -> new ReentrantLock());
    }

    public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // LOCK ORDERING: always lock the smaller account ID first
        String first = from.compareTo(to) < 0 ? from : to;
        String second = first.equals(from) ? to : from;

        getLock(first).lock();
        try {
            getLock(second).lock();
            try {
                BigDecimal fromBal = accounts.getOrDefault(from, BigDecimal.ZERO);
                BigDecimal toBal = accounts.getOrDefault(to, BigDecimal.ZERO);
                accounts.put(from, fromBal.subtract(amount));
                accounts.put(to, toBal.add(amount));
            } finally { getLock(second).unlock(); }
        } finally { getLock(first).unlock(); }
    }
    // High concurrency: SAV2001→CUR3001 does NOT block FD4001→SAV5001
    // Lock ordering: alphabetical → no circular wait → no deadlock
}
```

### How does lock ordering prevent deadlock?

Deadlock requires four conditions: mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, and circular wait. Lock ordering breaks circular wait: if all threads always acquire locks in the same global order, no thread can hold a higher-ordered lock while waiting for a lower-ordered one.

### What is the performance difference?

| Configuration | Throughput (transfers/sec) |
|---|---|
| Coarse (1 lock, 200 threads) | ~2,000 |
| Fine + ordering (10K locks, 200 threads) | ~45,000 |
| Improvement | 22x |

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How does ConcurrentHashMap use fine-grained locking? In Java 8+, it uses per-bucket synchronization: CAS for empty buckets, synchronized(firstNode) for non-empty buckets.
- Can lock-free replace fine-grained? For single-variable operations, yes (AtomicInteger). For multi-variable atomic operations (transfer: debit + credit), you still need locking.
